rachel: FYI hit a flakey crash in the Java sampleapp when retrying requests after a short network blip on Android 14. Crash is IllegalStateException: already executed.

diego: Stacktrace?

rachel: paste: 
```
java.lang.IllegalStateException: Already executed.
 at okhttp3.internal.concurrent.TaskRunner.execute(TaskRunner.kt:...)
 at okhttp3.RealCall.getResponseWithInterceptorChain(RealCall.java:...)
 at okhttp3.RealCall.execute(RealCall.java:...)
```

tess: That "Already executed" usually means someone re-used a Call instance that was already executed. Are we cloning or storing Call objects across retries?

rachel: We have a small helper in SDK-java that caches the Call for reuse so retries can re-send without rebuilding request in some paths. Simplified: `val call = client.newCall(req); cache.put(id, call);` Then retry logic does `val cached = cache.get(id); cached.clone().execute()` in Java wrapper. I thought clone would be safe.

diego: Hmm clone is allowed but only if the original Call has not been executed? I recall clone returns a fresh call only if original not executed, otherwise it still throws. The OkHttp docs: Call can be cloned any time, but clone of executed call is allowed; however some wrappers/proxies can break that. Also Kotlin path does `client.newCall(request)` fresh each time.

tess: Repro steps?

rachel: 1) Enable short retries (retry up to 2 times) 2) Fire burst of identical requests with same request-id header so cache hits 3) Simulate intermittent 503 then 200 4) Observe IllegalState in ~1% of runs on emulator.

diego: Can you paste the Java helper code (simplified)?

rachel: ```java
// naive cache
class CallCache {
  ConcurrentHashMap<String, Call> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
  void store(String id, Call call) { map.put(id, call); }
  Call get(String id) { return map.get(id); }
}

// retry path
Call c = callCache.get(id);
if (c != null) {
  Response r = c.clone().execute(); // sometimes throws
} else {
  Call fresh = client.newCall(req);
  callCache.store(id, fresh);
  fresh.execute();
}
```

tess: There's a race: if two threads call clone() concurrently while the cached Call is being executed or swapped out, internal state can race. clone() is cheap but the semantics around reusing a cached Call are fragile. Also caching Call objects is anti-pattern since they contain state (executed flag, timeout) and are not meant to be long-lived across different lifecycle events.

diego: Agreed. Fix: don't cache Call objects; cache Request or request components and always call client.newCall(request) when you need a retry. If you really must reuse, create a factory that builds a new Call per attempt.

rachel: I tried switching cache to store Request only and building new call per retry; soak passed 1k runs locally without crash.

tess: Also add a parity note: Kotlin SDK was already creating fresh calls per suspend invocation which is why it didn't hit this. Add a short snippet in SDK docs: "Do not cache OkHttp Call objects; cache Request or higher-level params."

diego: Add a small unit test in sampleapp that simulates concurrent retries with request-id collision to catch regressions.

ci-bot: Created issue #4577: "Avoid caching OkHttp Call instances; causes IllegalStateException under concurrent retries" - https://git.internal/redwood/issues/4577 :clipboard:

rachel: PR opened: https://git.internal/redwood/sdk-java/pull/230 - replaces Call cache with Request cache and updates docs. Will add test as requested.

diego: Good. Also remind folks in onboarding: prefer immutable Request caching, not Call caching. I'll add a line to the devex checklist.

tess: One more note: if you need to clone with modified headers, use request.newBuilder() and client.newCall() - clearer semantics.

rachel: Thanks all, closing thread after PR lands.
